  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1 DESCRIPTION...\n\nNot much change to earlier forecast philosophies. However, based\non the infusion of latest CAM and ensemble output, we feel a bit\nmore confidence in at least localized flash flooding across\nsouthwestern NH today and tonight. A flash flood watch has been\nissued to cover this possible threat.\n\nOverall, large scale mass fields remain largely unchanged in\nlatest model output for the next two days or so. The main issue\nhas been finer scale feature such as where training convection\nwill take place. Right now the most concern is for the eastern\nslopes of the southwestern NH hills and mountains. This is where\nthe highest PWATs will coincide with instability and an\nincreasing southeasterly LLJ (helps the upslope process).\nOverall, looking like basin averages of 1-2 inches there but\nmoderate chances for localized amounts up to 5 inches in \npersistent convective showers and thunderstorms. This will \nlikely lead to at least a localized flash flood threat, mainly \nthis afternoon and evening. \n\nAs for the rest of the the forecast area, fog is likely this\nmorning on the coastal plain. For this afternoon, expect\nincreasing chances for showers and perhaps a few thunderstorms\nwith locally heavy rainfall. However, the flood threat is much\nlower than southwestern NH. In place, many areas in central and\nmountainous ME may be dry for much of the day except the\naforementioned fog and possible drizzle.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2 DESCRIPTION...\n\nIt will be a slow rebound to warmer, drier conditions with the upper \n-level low still hanging overhead Thursday, but eventually it is \nejected out to sea by a 500 mb jet streak over the Gulf Stream. \nThere will at least be a chance for nuisance rain showers until the \nlow fully exits our region sometime Friday/Friday night, but with \nthe upper-level forcing collapsing as the surface low \noccludes/vertically stacks, the chances for more heavy rain will \ndecrease as time goes on. Thursday may bring some locally heavy rain \nearly, especially focused along the coast, but more widespread will \nbe areas of drizzle/mist, and generally raw conditions for July. \nSome mixed signals Friday, as there may be enough cold air and \nmoisture aloft with the remnants of the trough to fuel some diurnal \nshowers, but there is also the chance subtle height rises will \nresult in subsidence and dry conditions prevailing. Cool temperatures \nin the 60s on Thursday will increase to more seasonable 70s-low 80s \nby Friday.\n\nSaturday looks to generally be a rinse and repeat of Friday, with \nonly a isolated chance of diurnal/upslope-enhanced showers, and near \nseasonable temperatures. The calm weather is short lived, however, \nas sizable rain chances return by Sunday, especially for the \ninterior parts of the forecast area. Model spread begins to increase \nby this range, but there is agreement regardless that another slow-\nmoving, moisture-rich shortwave trough will impact the Northeast \nU.S. to start next week. The biggest disparity at this point is the \nECMWF indicating a full cutoff low over the Ohio Valley, while the \nGFS shows more of an open wave (but still slow moving) 500 mb \ntrough. Interests with outdoor activities should plan for rain in \nthe forecast Sunday-Monday.",
